{"batchcomplete": true, "query": {"pages": [{"pageid": 1968363, "ns": 0, "title": "Mapinguari", "extract": "The Mapinguari or Mapinguary is a mythological creature from Brazilian folklore. Referred to as the 'Brazilian Bigfoot' in popular media, the Mapinguari is often described as a man-eating monster that is extremely foul-smelling and hairy, with hook-shaped nails, backwards-facing feet, bipedal gait, a gaping mouth in its belly, and a single eye like a Cyclops.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:45:38Z", "lastrevid": 1367479969, "length": 36090, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mapinguari", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mapinguari&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mapinguari", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q605438"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/14/science/archaeology-sloths-human-migration.html"}, {"url": "https://skeptoid.com/episodes/4888"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/62860983"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=d7owDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA98"}, {"url": "https://www.scribd.com/document/421880062/Edentata-4"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=YUMSAQAAIAAJ&q=Mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=q1UNAQAAMAAJ&q=mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=-7vjAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA321"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=xXTYAAAAMAAJ&mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=aVUNAQAAMAAJ&q=Gorjala"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=aVUNAQAAMAAJ&q="}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=6NUsAQAAIAAJ&q=k%C3%BCm%C3%BCn"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0016-6987"}, {"url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/29787582"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=w6EzAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA223"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=w6EzAQAAMAAJ&pg=383"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=w6EzAQAAMAAJ&pg=136"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=OpoMAAAAYAAJ&mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=9-EMAAAAYAAJ&q=mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=N3kSAQAAIAAJ&q=mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=bc_KEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT59"}, {"url": "https://myweb.fsu.edu/jgaleano/pdf/amazonia2014.pdf"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?&id=KHMYAAAAYAAJ&q=mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=V_PiEAAAQBAJ&pg=PT113"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=c7AwDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA250"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=4q1uAAAAMAAJ&q=curupiras"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=zYg3eOqjq4UC&pg=PA131"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=pelGAAAAYAAJ&q=mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=zYg3eOqjq4UC&q=shaaroba"}, {"url": "https://www.scielo.org.ar/pdf/ava/n15/n15a06.pdf"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1590%2F1981.81222016000100011"}, {"url": "https://www.scielo.br/j/bgoeldi/a/SH8jW3RF4Z7zv8RQD57JSvk/?format=pdf&lang=pt"}, {"url": "https://repositorio.ufc.br/bitstream/riufc/3015/1/2011_dis_snrfbezerra.pdf"}, {"url": "http://repositorio.ufc.br/handle/riufc/3015"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=KdfaAAAAMAAJ&q=Ameghino"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=fk3PAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA324"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=TC0BEQAAQBAJ&pg=PT168"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=SOfWAAAAMAAJ&q=mapinguari"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1002%2Fjmor.21333"}, {"url": "https://www.seattletimes.com/nation-world/when-did-humans-first-occupy-the-americas-ask-the-sloth-bones/?amp=1"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=e-9ZAAAAMAAJ&q=%22nas+costas%22"}, {"url": "https://www.pbs.org/video/mapinguari-fearsome-beast-and-protector-of-the-amazon-xnse9d/"}, {"url": "https://news.climate.columbia.edu/2021/10/07/now-extinct-giant-south-american-sloth-likely-devoured-meat-with-its-vegetables/"}, {"url": "https://magazine.caltech.edu/post/ioc8k90zywsgmifedo7oh2de79csod"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=mbU-F42JU1AC"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=9d8MAAAAYAAJ&q=%22mapinguari+pelas%22"}, {"url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2007/07/08/world/americas/08amazon.html"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=Ik4TAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA6"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1080%2F08912963.2022.2035379"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0891-2963"}, {"url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/08912963.2022.2035379"}, {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2023HBio...35..284T"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:246698665"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.palaeo.2017.03.027"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.revpalbo.2021.104549"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1007%2Fs10914-012-9189-y"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/1064-7554"}, {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2017PPP...476...42S"}, {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2022RPaPa.29604549V"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:239902623"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:254701351"}, {"url": "https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0031018216306630"}, {"url": "http://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10914-012-9189-y"}, {"url": "https://hdl.handle.net/11336%2F69736"}]}, {"pageid": 11269393, "ns": 0, "title": "Marajoara culture", "extract": "The Marajoara or Marajó culture was an ancient pre-Cabraline era culture that flourished on Marajó island at the mouth of the Amazon River in northern Brazil. In a survey, Charles C. Mann suggests the culture appeared to flourish between 800 AD and 1400 AD, based on archeological studies. Researchers have documented that there was human activity at these sites as early as 1000 BC. The culture seems to have persisted into the colonial era.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:48:59Z", "lastrevid": 1361426534, "length": 34110, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marajoara_culture", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Marajoara_culture&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marajoara_culture", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2624476"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.marajoara.com/current_research.html"}, {"url": "http://www.americanindian.si.edu/searchcollections/results.aspx?regid=2910"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/149100char/page/326"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/lostcityofztaleo00gran/page/315"}, {"url": "http://www.dsr.inpe.br/marajo/Archaeological.pdf"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:83931819"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1086%2F272316"}, {"url": "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2449810"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=xeJMAgAAQBAJ&pg=RA1-PA431"}, {"url": "https://www.neperos.com/article/s57a1i7002ab74a5"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.18542%2Famazonica.v9i1.5497"}, {"url": "https://portalamazonia.com/estados/para/ceramica-marajoara-arte-que-resiste-ao-tempo"}, {"url": "https://periodicos.ufpa.br/index.php/amazonica/article/download/5497/4595"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/2176-0675"}, {"url": "https://www.olimpia.sp.gov.br/2a-arte-em-ceramica-marajoara"}, {"url": "https://parademinas.mg.gov.br/artistas-expoem-releitura-da-ceramica-marajoara-na-escola-de-artes/"}, {"url": "http://repositorio.ufpa.br:8080/jspui/handle/2011/7212"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/oclc/2541231"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20160303174749/http://www.dsr.inpe.br/marajo/Archaeological.pdf"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20150402131152/http://www.americanindian.si.edu/searchcollections/results.aspx?regid=2910"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20220307100631/http://repositorio.ufpa.br:8080/jspui/handle/2011/7212"}]}, {"pageid": 26748150, "ns": 0, "title": "Manteño-Huancavilca culture", "extract": "The Manteño-Huancavilca culture (Spanish: Los Manteños) were one of the last pre-Columbian cultures in modern-day Ecuador, active from 850 to 1600 CE (1150–400 BP).  It encompasses the area of the earlier Valdivia culture.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:53:04Z", "lastrevid": 1361599701, "length": 5921, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mante%C3%B1o-Huancavilca_culture", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mante%C3%B1o-Huancavilca_culture&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mante%C3%B1o-Huancavilca_culture", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q961245"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://alabado.org/en/pre-columbian-cultures/manteno-guancavilca"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=yZr-lxQgJiAC&pg=PA505"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=LnZn1kfWeL8C&pg=PA303"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20141006100211/http://alabado.org/en/pre-columbian-cultures/manteno-guancavilca"}]}, {"pageid": 27124443, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'allaqta, Castilla", "extract": "Mawk'allaqta, also Mawk'a Llaqta (Quechua mawk'a ancient, llaqta place (village, town, city, country, nation), \"ancient place\", Hispanicized spelling Maucallacta), is an architectural Inca complex above the contemporary mountain village of San Antonio (Pampacolca District, Castilla Province, Arequipa Region). It is located 3,700 meters above sea level, overlooking the neighbouring valley. The nearest bigger town, Pampacolca, is located approximately 170 kilometers (approx. 110 miles) north-west of the city Arequipa in the southern highlands of Peru.\nThe archaeological project \"Condesuyos\" has been carried out since 1996 by the Center for Pre-Columbian Studies (University of Warsaw, Poland) represented by its director, Prof. Dr. Mariusz Ziółkowski, and Universidad Católica Santa María (Arequipa, Peru) represented by Dr. Luis Augusto Belan Franco, the director of the University Archaeological Museum and Dr. Maximo Neira Avendaño – the scientific consultant for the project. The project is being carried out in co-operation with the Instituto Nacional de Cultura of Peru and covers archaeological investigation in the vicinity of the volcano Coropuna which was frequently mentioned by chroniclers of the 16th and 17th centuries as an oracle, worshipped since pre-Inca times. The architectural complex Mawk'allaqta, composed of more than two hundred stone buildings and tombs, an ushnu and three huge ceremonial platforms, made of stones and earth, may be considered the principal administrative, pilgrimage and religious centre related to the volcano and the most important Inca site discovered in Kuntisuyu, the fourth quarter of the Inca Empire. Mawk'allaqta is being investigated as part of a sub-project which forms part of the archaeological project \"Condesuyos\".\nMawk'allaqta was inaugurated and opened to visitors on August 29, 2009.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:53:10Z", "lastrevid": 1364716614, "length": 4002, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Castilla", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27allaqta,_Castilla&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Castilla", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15252211"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -15.68527778, "lon": -72.6225, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.maucallacta.com/"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Mawk'allaqta,_Castilla&params=15_41_07_S_72_37_21_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Mawk'allaqta,_Castilla&params=15_41_07_S_72_37_21_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 28215013, "ns": 0, "title": "Marpa, Peru", "extract": "Marpa is a ruined pre-Hispanic town located along the Cotahuasi Canyon in the Andes range of southern Peru.\nThe Cotahuasi River arises in the Wansu mountain range, cutting a route south-west and ending some 300 km later as the Ocoña River mouthing into the Pacific Ocean. Declared a \"Zona Reserva Turistica\" in 1988 very little is known about the canyon and Marpa. Rafting expeditions first ran the canyon in 1994. In pre-Hispanic times the Inca road along the canyon was well-used, and sections have survived, as have the ruins of agricultural terraces, staircases and ancient tombs. The Inca road may have been built along the route of an older road built by the Wari culture, a dominant force in the highlands of central and southern Peru between 500AD and 900AD.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:46:13Z", "lastrevid": 1364716523, "length": 2804, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marpa,_Peru", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Marpa,_Peru&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marpa,_Peru", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q6772445"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -15.24944444, "lon": -72.98888889, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.pioneerexpeditions.com/documents/Peru%202009.pdf"}, {"url": "http://www.responsibletravel.com/Trip/Trip901828.htm"}, {"url": "http://www.oregonkayaking.net/rivers/cotahuasi/cotahuasi.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20110715084718/http://www.pioneerexpeditions.com/documents/Peru%202009.pdf"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20100531053225/http://www.gonomad.com/womens/0710/peru-rafting.html"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20100224125448/http://www.responsibletravel.com/trip/Trip901828.htm"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Marpa,_Peru&params=15_14_58_S_72_59_20_W_"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20110616152321/http://www.photographersdirect.com/buyers/search.asp?search=Marpa&sz=0&maximages=40&l=on&p=on&s=on&w=on"}]}, {"pageid": 30227161, "ns": 0, "title": "Manuscript 512", "extract": "Manuscript 512 (Portuguese: Manuscrito 512) is a ten-page manuscript of dubious veracity and unknown authorship that relates the discovery of a \"lost city\" in Bahia, Brazil by a group of bandeirantes in 1753. Originally found in 1839 at the National Library of Brazil, where it is kept to this day, the document tells of a group of Portuguese adventurers who searched for a long time the legendary mines of Muribeca, traveling for about ten years in the Brazil's jungle. During their journey the adventurers discovered the abandoned settlement of a lost city whose architecture, monuments, and artifacts recall  Greco-Roman style. \nThe manuscript is one of the most famed documents of the National Library's collection and some Brazilian historians consider it \"the greatest myth of national archaeology\", while others praise its vivid and picturesque writing style. During the 19th and 20th centuries, Manuscript 512 was the object of intense debate and instigated many expeditions by adventurers and investigators, notably Sir Richard F. Burton, who published the work Highlands of Brazil in 1869, and Colonel Percy Harrison Fawcett, who disappeared on one of his \"Lost City of Z\" expeditions through inner Brazil, resulting in several attempts to find him. \nThe \"lost city\" described in the manuscript inspired several articles, films and novels, such as José de Alencar's As Minas de Prata (1865), Rider Haggard's King Solomon's Mines (1886), and Arthur Conan Doyle's The Lost World (1912). In addition, the character Indiana Jones may have been inspired by the events surrounding Colonel Fawcett.\nAccess to the original document is very restricted, but a digitized version is currently available online.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T10:13:17Z", "lastrevid": 1364244014, "length": 14971, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuscript_512", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Manuscript_512&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manuscript_512", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q2503683"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.scielo.br/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S0102-01882002000100008&lng=&nrm=iso&tlng="}, {"url": "https://www.bn.gov.br/explore/curiosidades/documento-512-mapa-uma-cidade-perdida"}, {"url": "http://www.uesc.br/eventos/iconlireanais/iconlire_anais/anais-52.pdf"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20210122125558/https://www.bn.gov.br/explore/curiosidades/documento-512-mapa-uma-cidade-perdida"}, {"url": "https://www.neperos.com/article/s57er10382c57546"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/losttrailslostci00fawc"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/mysteriesofancie035488mbp"}, {"url": "https://archive.org/details/explorationsofhi02burt"}]}, {"pageid": 30298153, "ns": 0, "title": "Marcahuamachuco", "extract": "Marcahuamachuco is an archeological site of Pre-Incan ruins in the La Libertad Region of Peru. Although less well-known than other sites, it is considered significant and has been referred to by archaeologists as \"Machu Picchu of the North\" and \"The Jewel of La Libertad.\"", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:46:26Z", "lastrevid": 1365369333, "length": 9896, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcahuamachuco", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Marcahuamachuco&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcahuamachuco", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q6755997"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -7.7853, "lon": -78.0783, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.deperu.com/arqueologia/huamach.html"}, {"url": "http://www.livinginperu.com/news/14140"}, {"url": "http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=sP/kF71z4hg="}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20110601225207/http://www.andina.com.pe/Ingles/Noticia.aspx?id=sP/kF71z4hg%3D"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Marcahuamachuco&params=7.7853_S_78.0783_W_region:PE_source:wikidata"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Marcahuamachuco&params=7.7853_S_78.0783_W_region:PE_source:wikidata_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 39009670, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'allaqta, Espinar", "extract": "Mawk'allaqta, also Mawk'a Llaqta (Quechua mawk'a ancient, llaqta place (village, town, city, country, nation), \"ancient place\", hispanicized spellings Maukallacta, Maukallaqta, Mauk'allaqta) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Cusco Region, Espinar Province, on the border of the districts Coporaque and Suykutambo. Mawk'allaqta is situated on the banks of the Hank'amayu and the Apurímac River at a height of 3,915 metres (12,844 ft).", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:12Z", "lastrevid": 1364852391, "length": 3134, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Espinar", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27allaqta,_Espinar&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Espinar", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15949707"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -14.925, "lon": -71.56611111, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.perutoptours.com/index07cu_coporaque_maukallaqta.html"}, {"url": "http://www.rupestreweb.info/espinar.html"}, {"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=2598"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140220073034/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=2598"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Mawk'allaqta,_Espinar&params=14_55_30_S_71_33_58_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Mawk'allaqta,_Espinar&params=14_55_30_S_71_33_58_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 39517707, "ns": 0, "title": "Marayniyoq", "extract": "Marayniyoq or Marayniyoc (possibly from Quechua maran, maray batan or grindstone, maray to tear down, to knock down) is an archaeological site in the region of Ayacucho in Peru. It lies southeast of the town of Huanta and southwest of Huamanguilla at the border of the Huanta Province, Huamanguilla District and the Huamanga Province, Pacaycasa District in a plain called Vega Pampa. It is considered a Wari site.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:14Z", "lastrevid": 1364716451, "length": 3548, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marayniyoq", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Marayniyoq&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marayniyoq", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15949853"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "https://futatraw.ourproject.org/descargas/DicQuechuaBolivia.pdf"}, {"url": "https://esmiperu.blogspot.de/2007/03/las-estructuras-mortuorias-de.html"}, {"url": "https://ficha.sigmincetur.mincetur.gob.pe/index.aspx?cod_Ficha=4017"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.18800%2Fboletindearqueologiapucp.200001.018"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:108925047"}]}, {"pageid": 41731709, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'allaqta, Melgar", "extract": "Mawk'allaqta, also Mawk'a Llaqta (Quechua mawk'a ancient, llaqta place (village, town, city, country, nation), \"ancient place\", Hispanicized spelling Maucallacta) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Puno Region, Melgar Province, Nuñoa District. The site was declared a National Cultural Heritage (Patrimonio Cultural) of Peru by the National Institute of Culture.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:41Z", "lastrevid": 1364852458, "length": 2580, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Melgar", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27allaqta,_Melgar&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Melgar", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15949967"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=470"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140204043034/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=470"}]}, {"pageid": 41731720, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'allaqta, Sandia", "extract": "Mawk'allaqta, also Mawk'a Llaqta, (Quechua mawk'a ancient, llaqta place (village, town, city, country, nation), \"ancient place\", Hispanicized spelling Maukallacta) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Puno Region, Sandia Province, Sandia District. The site was declared a National Cultural Heritage (Patrimonio Cultural) of Peru by the National Institute of Culture.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:31Z", "lastrevid": 1364852490, "length": 2607, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Sandia", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27allaqta,_Sandia&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Sandia", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15949970"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4762"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140204043145/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4762"}]}, {"pageid": 41798901, "ns": 0, "title": "Markansaya", "extract": "Markansaya or Hatun Markansaya (Quechua) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Apurímac Region, Cotabambas Province, Haquira District.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:42Z", "lastrevid": 1364846481, "length": 1520, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markansaya", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Markansaya&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markansaya", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15950022"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=583"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140218211637/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=583"}]}, {"pageid": 41816835, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'allaqta, Paruro", "extract": "Mawk'allaqta or Mawk'a Llaqta (Quechua mawk'a ancient, llaqta place (village, town, city, country, nation), \"ancient place\", hispanicized and mixed spellings Mauka Llacta, Mauka Llaqta, Maukallaqta) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Cusco Region, Paruro Province, Paccaritambo District, near Mullipampa (Mollebamba).", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:31Z", "lastrevid": 1364852477, "length": 3050, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Paruro", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27allaqta,_Paruro&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_Paruro", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15950042"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -13.70472222, "lon": -71.96805556, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=3618"}, {"url": "http://maukallaqtacusconet-abel.blogspot.de/"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140220065354/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=3618"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Mawk'allaqta,_Paruro&params=13_42_17_S_71_58_05_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Mawk'allaqta,_Paruro&params=13_42_17_S_71_58_05_W_type:landmark"}, {"url": "https://consultasenlinea.mincetur.gob.pe/fichaInventario/index.aspx?cod_Ficha=3618"}]}, {"pageid": 41872125, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'allaqta, La Unión", "extract": "Mawk'allaqta, also Mawk'a Llaqta (Quechua mawk'a ancient, llaqta place (village, town, city, country, nation), \"ancient place\", Hispanicized spelling Maukallakta) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Arequipa Region, La Unión Province, Puyca District, located about 2 kilometers from Puyca.\nMawk'allaqta is the largest and most important archaeological complex of the Cotahuasi valley. Developed by the Wari culture, it was strategically located on trading routes. Later it was occupied by the Allcca and later still by the Inca, who used it as a center of religious and military administration in the valley. In the 21st century, the complex is included within the Cotahuasi Subbasin Landscape Reserve.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:32Z", "lastrevid": 1364852440, "length": 4180, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_La_Uni%C3%B3n", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27allaqta,_La_Uni%C3%B3n&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27allaqta,_La_Uni%C3%B3n", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q15950057"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4089"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140224110036/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=4089"}]}, {"pageid": 42033517, "ns": 0, "title": "Mapuche history", "extract": "As an archaeological culture, the Mapuche people of southern Chile and Argentina have a long history which dates back to 600–500 BC. The Mapuche society underwent great transformations after Spanish contact in the mid–16th century. These changes included the adoption of Old World crops and animals and the onset of a rich Spanish–Mapuche trade in La Frontera and Valdivia. Despite these contacts Mapuche were never completely subjugated by the Spanish Empire. Between the 18th and 19th century Mapuche culture and people spread eastwards into the Pampas and the Patagonian plains. This vast new territory allowed Mapuche groups to control a substantial part of the salt and cattle trade in the Southern Cone.\nBetween 1861 and 1883 the Republic of Chile conducted a series of campaigns that ended Mapuche independence causing the death of thousands of Mapuche through combat, pillaging, starvation and smallpox epidemics. Argentina conducted similar campaigns on the eastern side of the Andes in the 1870s. In large parts of the Mapuche lands the traditional economy collapsed forcing thousands to seek themselves to the large cities and live in impoverished conditions as housemaids, hawkers or labourers.\nFrom the late 20th century onwards Mapuche people have been increasingly active in conflicts over land rights and indigenous rights.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-04T08:10:40Z", "lastrevid": 1367271564, "length": 90029, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mapuche_history", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mapuche_history&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mapuche_history", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q17078154"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.livescience.com/history/070604_polynesian_chicken.html"}, {"url": "http://www.uta.cl/masma/patri_edu/PDF/LeyIndigena.PDF"}, {"url": "http://www.cooperativa.cl/p4_noticias/antialone.html?page=http://www.cooperativa.cl/p4_noticias/site/artic/20051111/pags/20051111124708.html"}, {"url": "http://www.speroforum.com/a/20549/Mapuche-struggle-for-autonomy-in-Chile"}, {"url": "http://www.soundsandcolours.com/articles/chile/mapuche-hunger-strike-in-chile-highlights-the-real-problem-facing-president-sebastian-pinera/"}, {"url": "http://www.censo.cl/"}, {"url": "http://www.indec.gov.ar/webcenso/ECPI/index_ecpi.asp"}, {"url": "http://www.estudios-atacamenos.ucn.cl/web%20est%20atac%2037/pdf/numero04/10Dans.pdf"}, {"url": "http://www.dendrocronologia.cl/pubs/2007_Dillehay(AncientCultivatedWetlands).pdf"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/602/w3-article-691.html"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/602/w3-article-721.html"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/602/w3-article-94304.html"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/temas/index.asp?id_ut=ocupaciondelaaraucaniaenelchilerepublicano(1860-1883)"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/temas/dest.asp?id=ocupacionestafas"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/602/w3-article-714.html"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/602/w3-article-94620.html"}, {"url": "http://www.scielo.cl/scielo.php?pid=S0717-71942011000200004&script=sci_arttext"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/602/w3-article-3615.html"}, {"url": "http://etd.library.vanderbilt.edu/available/etd-07242006-180102/unrestricted/Dissertation.pdf"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/archivos2/pdfs/MC0009042.pdf"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/archivos2/pdfs/mc0008625.pdf"}, {"url": "http://diario.elmercurio.cl/detalle/index.asp?id=%7B09ab1961-e32e-467b-8887-fdddcf674b58%7D"}, {"url": "https://books.google.com/books?id=ikKkYgEACAAJ"}, {"url": "https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B6Tbgy2OaGNyd3pnMGl4dHA4VGs/view"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.cl/temas/index.asp?id_ut=laencomienda.siglosxvi-xviii"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140304021124/http://www.estudios-atacamenos.ucn.cl/web%20est%20atac%2037/pdf/numero04/10Dans.pdf"}, {"url": "https://revistas.uchile.cl/index.php/CDH/article/download/46464/48492"}, {"url": "http://www.anales.uchile.cl/index.php/ANUC/article/viewFile/23061/24411"}, {"url": "http://www.turismocientifico.cl/admin/apps/filemanager/repository/%C3%A1reas%20del%20conocimiento/Poblamiento,%20historia%20y%20cultura/Antropologia/Etnias/Al%20Pueblo%20Chono.pdf"}, {"url": "https://semanariorepublicano.uchile.cl/index.php/ANUC/article/download/19032/20162"}, {"url": "http://www.memoriachilena.gob.cl/archivos2/pdfs/MC0012923.pdf"}, {"url": "https://revistas.unlp.edu.ar/raab/article/view/161"}, {"url": "http://intushistoria.uai.cl/index.php/intushistoria/article/view/122/108"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20080611004448/http://www.indec.gov.ar/webcenso/ECPI/index_ecpi.asp"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20081030080729/http://www.uta.cl/masma/patri_edu/PDF/LeyIndigena.PDF"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:14709971"}, {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2007PNAS..10410335S"}, {"url": "https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2010Sci...328.1344L"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:143718237"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:59480757"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:157067428"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20130928044705/http://www.cooperativa.cl/p4_noticias/antialone.html?page=http://www.cooperativa.cl/p4_noticias/site/artic/20051111/pags/20051111124708.html"}, {"url": "https://scielo.conicyt.cl/scielo.php?pid=S0718-10432021000100309&script=sci_arttext&tlng=en"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20181101072100/http://www.upla.cl/noticias/2017/01/19/de-la-polinesia-a-tunquen-evidencias-de-mestizaje-con-poblacion-local/"}, {"url": "http://www.upla.cl/noticias/2017/01/19/de-la-polinesia-a-tunquen-evidencias-de-mestizaje-con-poblacion-local/"}, {"url": "https://api.semanticscholar.org/CorpusID:244279716"}, {"url": "https://www.science.org/doi/full/10.1126/science.328.5984.1344"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1007%2Fs11033-013-2509-3"}, {"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23666052"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.humimm.2019.04.015"}, {"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31101374"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1086%2F465556"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0717-73562017005000028"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.22199%2Fs07181043.1976.0004.00010"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0718-48832015000200004"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1017%2FS0003598X00096034"}, {"url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1965514"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1073%2Fpnas.0703993104"}, {"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17556540"}, {"url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2492461"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1073%2Fpnas.0801991105"}, {"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18663216"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1126%2Fscience.328.5984.1344"}, {"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20538927"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0034-98871998000700002"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.22199%2Fissn.0718-1043-2021-0028"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2FS0717-71942016000100004"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.15691%2F07176864.2014.094"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.4067%2Fs0716-078x2004000400009"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20120428171505/http://www.artehistoria.jcyl.es/cronicas/contextos/10876.htm"}, {"url": "http://www.artehistoria.jcyl.es/cronicas/contextos/10876.htm"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20080524114821/http://www.artehistoria.jcyl.es/cronicas/contextos/10888.htm"}, {"url": "http://www.artehistoria.jcyl.es/cronicas/contextos/10888.htm"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1111%2Fblar.12481"}, {"url": "https://doi.org/10.1215%2F00141801-7217365"}, {"url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/blar.12481"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0261-3050"}, {"url": "https://search.worldcat.org/issn/0014-1801"}, {"url": "https://read.dukeupress.edu/ethnohistory/article/66/1/117/137183/Between-the-Lof-and-the-Liberators-Mapuche"}, {"url": "https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/italian-english/parlamento"}, {"url": "https://hdl.handle.net/1983%2F67208c7c-da1d-4774-83a5-60cb342fa4a8"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20141129160347/http://www.soundsandcolours.com/articles/chile/mapuche-hunger-strike-in-chile-highlights-the-real-problem-facing-president-sebastian-pinera/"}]}, {"pageid": 42042948, "ns": 0, "title": "Mawk'ataray", "extract": "Mawk'ataray or Mawk'a Taray (Quechua mawk'a ancient, Taray a town, \"old Taray\", Hispanicized spelling Maukataray) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is located in the Cusco Region, Calca Province, Taray District.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:46Z", "lastrevid": 1364716619, "length": 1729, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27ataray", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mawk%27ataray&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mawk%27ataray", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q17078468"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=3635"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140306093456/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=3635"}]}, {"pageid": 42263954, "ns": 0, "title": "Markapukyu", "extract": "Markapukyu (Hispanicized spelling Marcapuquio) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is situated in the Pasco Region, Pasco Province, Yarusyacán District, at a height of 3,787 metres (12,425 ft). The name is derived from Quechua marka, meaning 'village' or 'storey', and pukyu, meaning 'spring of water',", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T17:56:49Z", "lastrevid": 1364716508, "length": 2184, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markapukyu", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Markapukyu&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markapukyu", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q17078288"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -10.4904, "lon": -76.1965, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=7159"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140322001743/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=7159"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Markapukyu&params=10.4904_S_76.1965_W_source:wikidata"}]}, {"pageid": 42514933, "ns": 0, "title": "Markahirka", "extract": "Markahirka or Marka Hirka (Quechua marka village, Ancash Quechua hirka mountain, \"village mountain\", Hispanicized spellings Marca Jirca, Marcajirca) is an archaeological site with cave paintings and stone tombs (chullpa) on a mountain of the same name in Peru. It is located in the Ancash Region, Huari Province, in the districts of Cajay and Masin. It is situated at a height of 3,580 metres (11,745 ft).  Markahirka is also a good viewpoint with views into the Puchka valley (Puchca) and to the towns of Huari (Wari) and Huachis (Wachis).", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:35Z", "lastrevid": 1364716498, "length": 2803, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Markahirka&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markahirka", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q16894681"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -9.37073611, "lon": -77.12986111, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=1706"}, {"url": "http://www.ign.gob.pe/nomenclatorDigital/carta.php?id=19572"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20140419012044/http://www.mincetur.gob.pe/TURISMO/OTROS/inventario%20turistico/Ficha.asp?cod_Ficha=1706"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20150924033524/http://www.ign.gob.pe/nomenclatorDigital/carta.php?id=19572"}, {"url": "https://futatraw.ourproject.org/descargas/DicQuechuaBolivia.pdf"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Markahirka&params=9_22_14.65_S_77_7_47.50_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Markahirka&params=9_22_14.65_S_77_7_47.50_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 45390685, "ns": 0, "title": "Maray Qalla", "extract": "Maray Qalla (Quechua maran, maray batan or grindstone, maray to tear down, to knock down, qalla carved stone, cobblestone; circular spindle disk; cheek, also spelled Maraycalla, Maraycalle) is an archaeological site in Peru. It is situated in the Ancash Region, Carlos Fermín Fitzcarrald Province. Maray Qalla lies at a section of the Qhapaq Ñan, the Inca road system.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T02:47:46Z", "lastrevid": 1364716442, "length": 2277, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maray_Qalla", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Maray_Qalla&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maray_Qalla", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q16473372"}, "coordinates": [{"lat": -9.07861111, "lon": -77.23972222, "primary": true, "globe": "earth"}], "extlinks": [{"url": "http://www.qhapaqnan.gob.pe/wordpress/?p=4990"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20150213190335/http://www.qhapaqnan.gob.pe/wordpress/?p=4990"}, {"url": "https://futatraw.ourproject.org/descargas/DicQuechuaBolivia.pdf"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Maray_Qalla&params=9_04_43_S_77_14_23_W_"}, {"url": "https://geohack.toolforge.org/geohack.php?pagename=Maray_Qalla&params=9_04_43_S_77_14_23_W_type:landmark"}]}, {"pageid": 58914638, "ns": 0, "title": "Mayo-Chinchipe", "extract": "The Mayo-Chinchipe or Mayo-Chinchipe-Marañon culture existed from c. 5500 – 1700 BCE in the highlands of what is now Ecuador and Peru, along the eastern slopes of the Amazonian Andes.", "contentmodel": "wikitext", "pagelanguage": "en", "pagelanguagehtmlcode": "en", "pagelanguagedir": "ltr", "touched": "2026-08-06T18:01:16Z", "lastrevid": 1351619884, "length": 3399, "fullurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayo-Chinchipe", "editurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mayo-Chinchipe&action=edit", "canonicalurl": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mayo-Chinchipe", "pageprops": {"wikibase_item": "Q58216521"}, "extlinks": [{"url": "http://palanda.arqueo-ecuatoriana.ec/complejo-cultural-mayo-chinchipe"}, {"url": "https://www.sciencealert.com/forgotten-origins-chocolate-mesoamerica-ecuador-theobroma-cacao-santa-ana-la-florida-ancient-mayo-chinchipe-culture"}, {"url": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/tentativelists/6091/"}, {"url": "https://www.theguardian.com/science/2018/oct/29/origin-of-chocolate-shifts-1400-miles-and-1500-years-cacao-ecuador"}, {"url": "https://palanda.arqueo-ecuatoriana.ec/complejo-cultural-mayo-chinchipe"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20141129074259/http://palanda.arqueo-ecuatoriana.ec/complejo-cultural-mayo-chinchipe"}, {"url": "https://web.archive.org/web/20181030171850/https://palanda.arqueo-ecuatoriana.ec/complejo-cultural-mayo-chinchipe"}]}]}, "limits": {"coordinates": 500, "extlinks": 500}}